3
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:13,000
And they know how to get you great gear and great prizes for all your camping adventures.

4
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:22,000
So go visit them at conahoecreakcom, at conohocreakcom.

5
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:27,000
And now to your scout master.

6
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:37,000
It's that time of year again. summer camp is right around the corner, and I have learned-

7
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:43,000
I have learned, folks- the secret of summer camp.

8
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:51,000
Summer camp is the art of learning how to be miserable and liking it.

9
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:55,000
We're all going to be uncomfortable at one point or another in our lives,

10
00:00:55,000 --> 00:00:59,000
So we better be learned to be cheerful in miserable conditions at summer camp.

11
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:02,000
That's a great place to do it, isn't it?

12
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:06,000
Yes, sir, This is podcast number 175..

13
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:38,000
Welcome back to the Scout Master Podcast. This is Clark Green.

14
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:42,000
Let's take a look at the mail bag Got a couple of people wrote in this week.

15
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:45,000
Hey, we heard from Michael Harrington and he had this to say about our last podcast

16
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:52,000
where we talked with our friend Arlam Ward and we got an update on his new troop out there in Colorado.

17
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:57,000
Michael said this podcast just addressed in a nutshell all of our troop experiences

18
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:01,000
and all of our challenges during patrol leaders council meetings

19
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:07,000
and troop meetings and camp outs and all of the adult involvement.

20
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:12,000
We're in Massachusetts, yet if you remove the names and places, I would have bet my bottom dollar

21
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:17,000
you were talking about our troop. It's amazing, isn't it?

22
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:21,000
We all go through the same stuff. That's why it's so much fun, right?

23
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:30,000
That's why sometimes this whole conversation and the podcast and the blog and things like that is useful,

24
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:35,000
because we're all doing the same thing, We all run into the same difficulties,

25
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:42,000
We all celebrate the same achievements, and it's just what makes the world go round, isn't it?

26
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:45,000
Daniel Desjardins wrote in to say:

27
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:49,000
my two sons are in a new troop because we moved

28
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:55,000
At this time. most of the troop is run by adults. The scout master, although, is retiring in October

29
00:02:55,000 --> 00:02:58,000
and I will have the pleasure to take over the position.

30
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:02,000
Congratulations, Daniel. Jump into the volcano. It's fine.

31
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:08,000
I've been scouting for a long time, but this will be the first time I attempt being a scout master.

32
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:11,000
I have been listening and reading everything you've done

33
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:15,000
and one thing that I will put in place will be a patrol leader's council,

34
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:19,000
and we're going to have the scouts be in charge just like they ought to do.

35
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:22,000
Keep on scouting. People like you make a difference.

36
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:26,000
Well, thank you, Daniel. Thank you for your kind words of encouragement. there

37
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:29,000
You're going to have the opportunity to go ahead and give this stuff a go

38
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:34,000
and see how it works- and it works. It works, Trust me.

39
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:38,000
It won't look like it's working at first, but it will work for you.

40
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:42,000
It's summertime. We're still keeping busy at ScoutMasterCGcom.

41
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:46,000
I posted a video this past week about an axe and a knife and a sharpening stone

42
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:48,000
that you would want to take a look at.

43
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:52,000
So go to our YouTube channel, ScoutMasterCG. on YouTube

44
00:03:52,000 --> 00:03:55,000
and, you know, check out the review of those items on the blog.

45
00:03:55,000 --> 00:03:59,000
They came from our friends at ConahoeCreekcom,

46
00:03:59,000 --> 00:04:01,000
So you want to make sure to check those out.

47
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:03,000
I think they're well worth your attention.

48
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:07,000
Well, on this Scout Master podcast and Scout Master ship, in seven minutes or less.

49
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:10,000
it's never, ever seven minutes

50
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:12,000
And it's certainly not less than that.

51
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:15,000
But you know, oh well, we're going to talk about summer camp,

52
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:17,000
because that's where we're all headed.

53
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:21,000
I have some advice for you about that and we've got an email question to answer,

54
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:23,000
and that's going to do it for this podcast.

55
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:25,000
So let's get started, shall we?

56
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:30,000
Scout Master ship in seven minutes or less.

57
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:39,000
It won't be too long before we're all packed up

58
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:41,000
and ready to take our big crowd of Scouts to summer camp.

59
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:44,000
I am looking forward to it, as I do every summer.

60
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:49,000
This will be summer number 29, or maybe 29..

61
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:51,000
Yeah, I think summer number 29..

62
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:55,000
A lot of those summers my role at summer camp was being on staff

63
00:04:55,000 --> 00:04:58,000
instead of being the Scout Master for our troop.

64
00:04:58,000 --> 00:05:01,000
I did that for about a dozen years in different senior positions

65
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:05,000
and a couple of those were as the camp director.

66
00:05:05,000 --> 00:05:09,000
That many years of working at camp and the couple of years being a camp director

67
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:12,000
and keeping up with our current staff-

68
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:16,000
And I can tell you something: things haven't changed a great deal.

69
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:21,000
The program, oh, that changes a little bit and you know different people come,

70
00:05:21,000 --> 00:05:25,000
but the same issues are the perennial issues,

71
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:28,000
that kind of make or break your week at summer camp.

72
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:33,000
We've attended the same summer camp since the troop was first chartered in the 1940s.

73
00:05:33,000 --> 00:05:38,000
Some of you like to go to different camps every year, try out different things, you know,

74
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:40,000
and that's all good.

75
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:42,000
I don't think there's a right or a wrong way to do it.

76
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:45,000
The right thing is getting out for a week at summer camp

77
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:52,000
After all those years on staff and having spent a long time as a Scout Master at camp

78
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:57,000
and watching you know literally thousands of Scout leaders

79
00:05:57,000 --> 00:06:02,000
and tens of thousands of Scouts go through a summer camp week.

80
00:06:02,000 --> 00:06:08,000
I can tell you- I believe authoritatively- that these are the most important things

81
00:06:08,000 --> 00:06:14,000
for you to be thinking about as an adult who's going to spend a week at camp.

82
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:23,000
The number one thing is: don't overdo it and kind of make yourself into a time bomb. okay,

83
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:29,000
If you get hungry or hot and tired and thirsty, you need to pay attention to that

84
00:06:29,000 --> 00:06:34,000
and you need to get cool and rested and fed and hydrated.

85
00:06:34,000 --> 00:06:39,000
I know I get pretty cranky if I don't look after myself,

86
00:06:39,000 --> 00:06:45,000
and years of observation prove that many of my fellow Scouts share the same problem.

87
00:06:45,000 --> 00:06:49,000
When you're going to spend a week at camp with a group of Scouts,

88
00:06:49,000 --> 00:06:52,000
the temptation is going to be to try to keep up with them.

89
00:06:52,000 --> 00:06:56,000
Now, more power to you if you can do that and not feel any effects.

90
00:06:56,000 --> 00:07:01,000
but most of us aren't 16 anymore, right?

91
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:04,000
So we have to look after ourselves and looking after yourself.

92
00:07:04,000 --> 00:07:11,000
most importantly, that you get hydrated and rested is really, really important,

93
00:07:11,000 --> 00:07:16,000
because if you turn yourself into this kind of ticking time bomb

94
00:07:16,000 --> 00:07:18,000
so that by the end of the week you have no patience left

95
00:07:18,000 --> 00:07:21,000
and you're getting a little touchy and you're getting kind of cranky.

96
00:07:21,000 --> 00:07:23,000
it's going to make everybody's life miserable.

97
00:07:23,000 --> 00:07:26,000
So that's probably that's the number one thing

98
00:07:26,000 --> 00:07:28,000
and that's why it's at the top of the list.

99
00:07:28,000 --> 00:07:34,000
Number two: I can tell you that the camp staff is totally dedicated

100
00:07:34,000 --> 00:07:37,000
to making your stay at camp worthwhile.

101
00:07:37,000 --> 00:07:39,000
I can tell you that

102
00:07:39,000 --> 00:07:41,000
I know that I've been to National Camp School-

103
00:07:41,000 --> 00:07:44,000
I think I went to five different years of that-

104
00:07:44,000 --> 00:07:48,000
and I know people from camps all over the country

105
00:07:48,000 --> 00:07:51,000
and I know that they put their heart into it.

106
00:07:51,000 --> 00:07:54,000
None of them are in it for the money-

107
00:07:54,000 --> 00:07:56,000
The money is not good-

108
00:07:56,000 --> 00:08:00,000
but they're there because they want to make this a great experience

109
00:08:00,000 --> 00:08:04,000
for the scouts and, in turn, for the adults

110
00:08:04,000 --> 00:08:06,000
that are accompanying the scouts there.

111
00:08:06,000 --> 00:08:09,000
One of the great experiences at a summer camp

112
00:08:09,000 --> 00:08:12,000
are the younger people who are on the staff of the camp.

113
00:08:12,000 --> 00:08:16,000
They're learning and growing, just like your scouts are,

114
00:08:16,000 --> 00:08:19,000
and you need to be considerate and supportive of them.

115
00:08:19,000 --> 00:08:24,000
That is a really important way to make life better while you're at camp:

116
00:08:24,000 --> 00:08:27,000
to be considerate and supportive of the staff,

117
00:08:27,000 --> 00:08:29,000
because that's what they're trying to do for you.

118
00:08:29,000 --> 00:08:32,000
Number three: no matter how much you think they deserve it,

119
00:08:32,000 --> 00:08:37,000
never berate or criticize or argue with or yell at

120
00:08:37,000 --> 00:08:41,000
a staff member, a youth leader or a fellow adult leader

121
00:08:41,000 --> 00:08:43,000
in the presence of your scouts.

122
00:08:43,000 --> 00:08:45,000
If it really needs to happen,

123
00:08:45,000 --> 00:08:49,000
a quiet talk at an appropriate distance, out of earshot from scouts,

124
00:08:49,000 --> 00:08:51,000
will usually solve the problem

125
00:08:51,000 --> 00:08:54,000
and a lot of times- if you're like me,

126
00:08:54,000 --> 00:08:56,000
you're going to find out- the problem might be yours.

127
00:08:56,000 --> 00:09:00,000
Number four is not to pull rank.

128
00:09:00,000 --> 00:09:03,000
You should understand what that means.

129
00:09:03,000 --> 00:09:05,000
Let's say you're a scout master

130
00:09:05,000 --> 00:09:07,000
and you go down to camp with your troop.

131
00:09:07,000 --> 00:09:08,000
Well, you're a scout master,

132
00:09:08,000 --> 00:09:11,000
You're not a general or an admiral or the president.

133
00:09:11,000 --> 00:09:15,000
so there really isn't any rank for you to pull, is there?

134
00:09:15,000 --> 00:09:20,000
Most people instantly respect the position that adults occupy

135
00:09:20,000 --> 00:09:21,000
at camp.

136
00:09:21,000 --> 00:09:24,000
The staff respects it and the scouts respect it.

137
00:09:24,000 --> 00:09:27,000
So don't make a big giant deal out of it.

138
00:09:27,000 --> 00:09:29,000
Don't ask for special privileges

139
00:09:29,000 --> 00:09:33,000
or try to impress staff members with yourself importance

140
00:09:33,000 --> 00:09:37,000
or your encyclopedic knowledge of the things that they are doing.

141
00:09:37,000 --> 00:09:39,000
They're very proud of what they are doing.

142
00:09:39,000 --> 00:09:41,000
They're working hard to do it

143
00:09:41,000 --> 00:09:43,000
and you going in there

144
00:09:43,000 --> 00:09:45,000
and telling them everything you know about it

145
00:09:45,000 --> 00:09:48,000
is not necessarily going to be very welcome.

146
00:09:48,000 --> 00:09:52,000
I'm in a situation where I used to be the camp director at the camp

147
00:09:52,000 --> 00:09:54,000
and I used to be on staff.

148
00:09:54,000 --> 00:09:56,000
I've been going there longer than any staff member has

149
00:09:56,000 --> 00:09:58,000
right at this point,

150
00:09:58,000 --> 00:10:02,000
but I don't expect to be treated much differently

151
00:10:02,000 --> 00:10:04,000
than any other scout leader.

152
00:10:04,000 --> 00:10:06,000
It's important that I keep that attitude

153
00:10:06,000 --> 00:10:08,000
and that I play the game

154
00:10:08,000 --> 00:10:10,000
and I go by the rules

155
00:10:10,000 --> 00:10:13,000
and I don't expect any special favors.

156
00:10:13,000 --> 00:10:15,000
Number five is: join in the program

157
00:10:15,000 --> 00:10:17,000
and encourage your scouts to join in too.

158
00:10:17,000 --> 00:10:19,000
If you travel around,

159
00:10:19,000 --> 00:10:21,000
if you go to a different camp every year,

160
00:10:21,000 --> 00:10:23,000
you're going to hit a program that you don't like-

161
00:10:23,000 --> 00:10:25,000
It's almost inevitable-

162
00:10:25,000 --> 00:10:27,000
and you're going to hit a program

163
00:10:27,000 --> 00:10:29,000
that maybe, in your humble opinion, isn't all that great

164
00:10:29,000 --> 00:10:31,000
and a staff that's not all that great.

165
00:10:31,000 --> 00:10:34,000
Well, I got to tell you from the staff side of things:

166
00:10:34,000 --> 00:10:38,000
a lively, appreciative, supportive audience

167
00:10:38,000 --> 00:10:40,000
does more than anything

168
00:10:40,000 --> 00:10:43,000
to enliven and energize a camp staff.

169
00:10:43,000 --> 00:10:45,000
Just being nice,

170
00:10:45,000 --> 00:10:47,000
You know,

171
00:10:47,000 --> 00:10:49,000
Even when things are going wrong,

172
00:10:49,000 --> 00:10:51,000
even when you think things are not great

173
00:10:51,000 --> 00:10:54,000
and maybe even that you're being mistreated,

174
00:10:54,000 --> 00:10:56,000
it doesn't cost much to be nice.

175
00:10:56,000 --> 00:11:00,000
There is a nominal kind of customer relationship, right,

176
00:11:00,000 --> 00:11:02,000
You plunk down money

177
00:11:02,000 --> 00:11:04,000
and your scouts plunk down money to go to camp

178
00:11:04,000 --> 00:11:06,000
and you expect a certain level of service

179
00:11:06,000 --> 00:11:08,000
and accommodation and things like that.

180
00:11:08,000 --> 00:11:10,000
Well, you know, let's remember,

181
00:11:10,000 --> 00:11:13,000
a scout camp is not club med

182
00:11:13,000 --> 00:11:16,000
and a scout camp is not necessarily going to be

183
00:11:16,000 --> 00:11:19,000
a highly polished professional atmosphere

184
00:11:19,000 --> 00:11:22,000
of, you know, absolute excellence.

185
00:11:22,000 --> 00:11:24,000
Camp staffs go through cycles.

186
00:11:24,000 --> 00:11:27,000
They have down years and they have up years

187
00:11:27,000 --> 00:11:30,000
and camps go through cycles like that.

188
00:11:30,000 --> 00:11:33,000
You know, the whole idea in all of this

189
00:11:33,000 --> 00:11:35,000
that I am saying is being a little humble

190
00:11:35,000 --> 00:11:37,000
and being cooperative

191
00:11:37,000 --> 00:11:39,000
and not complaining and grousing

192
00:11:39,000 --> 00:11:42,000
and being kind of that disgruntled customer.

193
00:11:42,000 --> 00:11:45,000
If you have a very bad experience,

194
00:11:45,000 --> 00:11:47,000
you'll want to talk to somebody.

195
00:11:47,000 --> 00:11:48,000
but I got to tell you

196
00:11:48,000 --> 00:11:50,000
the 15-year-old staff member,

197
00:11:50,000 --> 00:11:52,000
the guys that are working at camp,

198
00:11:52,000 --> 00:11:55,000
they're not the ones that are going to be able to change things.

199
00:11:55,000 --> 00:11:57,000
You want to get in touch with somebody

200
00:11:57,000 --> 00:11:58,000
at the council office.

201
00:11:58,000 --> 00:12:01,000
You want to talk to the camp director. maybe,

202
00:12:01,000 --> 00:12:03,000
like I said, your attitude

203
00:12:03,000 --> 00:12:06,000
is going to have a great deal to do

204
00:12:06,000 --> 00:12:09,000
with the quality of your experience.

205
00:12:09,000 --> 00:12:12,000
Number six is to participate

206
00:12:12,000 --> 00:12:14,000
in ceremonies and traditions

207
00:12:14,000 --> 00:12:15,000
and practices of the camp,

208
00:12:15,000 --> 00:12:17,000
even if they're not very familiar to you.

209
00:12:17,000 --> 00:12:19,000
and you know, I'll say again,

210
00:12:19,000 --> 00:12:22,000
even if you really don't care for them all that much.

211
00:12:22,000 --> 00:12:26,000
A camp staff is very proud of its traditions.

212
00:12:26,000 --> 00:12:29,000
Imposing your way of doing things

213
00:12:29,000 --> 00:12:31,000
on a camp staff,

214
00:12:31,000 --> 00:12:33,000
imposing your traditions on them,

215
00:12:33,000 --> 00:12:35,000
is really kind of rude.

216
00:12:35,000 --> 00:12:37,000
It's like going into somebody else's house

217
00:12:37,000 --> 00:12:39,000
rearranging the furniture.

218
00:12:39,000 --> 00:12:40,000
Play along.

219
00:12:40,000 --> 00:12:43,000
There's going to be a specific way

220
00:12:43,000 --> 00:12:45,000
that a camp- take you know,

221
00:12:45,000 --> 00:12:47,000
has a retreat ceremony

222
00:12:47,000 --> 00:12:49,000
and that's going to be a tradition that they've had

223
00:12:49,000 --> 00:12:51,000
and developed over a number of years

224
00:12:51,000 --> 00:12:53,000
and it's what people are used to

225
00:12:53,000 --> 00:12:55,000
and you know it. may

226
00:12:55,000 --> 00:12:57,000
there may be little details

227
00:12:57,000 --> 00:12:59,000
of it that rub you the wrong way,

228
00:12:59,000 --> 00:13:01,000
but listen, play along,

229
00:13:01,000 --> 00:13:02,000
Have a good time.

230
00:13:02,000 --> 00:13:04,000
You know the way that they do a campfire

231
00:13:04,000 --> 00:13:07,000
and the way things are done in the dining hall,

232
00:13:07,000 --> 00:13:11,000
the way that merit badge sessions are set up.

233
00:13:11,000 --> 00:13:13,000
go along with it and do your best

234
00:13:13,000 --> 00:13:16,000
to encourage your scouts to do the same.

235
00:13:16,000 --> 00:13:18,000
Number seven: you want to keep an eye

236
00:13:18,000 --> 00:13:21,000
on any kind of inner troop rivalries

237
00:13:21,000 --> 00:13:23,000
that may develop.

238
00:13:23,000 --> 00:13:25,000
You know, for the most part,

239
00:13:25,000 --> 00:13:27,000
your scouts are going to be out and about

240
00:13:27,000 --> 00:13:29,000
pretty much independently during the day

241
00:13:29,000 --> 00:13:31,000
and they're going to be running into other scouts

242
00:13:31,000 --> 00:13:33,000
and you have absolutely no control

243
00:13:33,000 --> 00:13:35,000
over what other scouts are going to do

244
00:13:35,000 --> 00:13:38,000
and you have, you know, only a little bit of control

245
00:13:38,000 --> 00:13:40,000
over what your scouts are going to do

246
00:13:40,000 --> 00:13:43,000
or say. and boys are boys

247
00:13:43,000 --> 00:13:45,000
and they get real competitive

248
00:13:45,000 --> 00:13:47,000
and sometimes that can lead to things

249
